## Overview

`httpapi-mcp` registers the shared HTTP API toolkit through Effect's native `McpServer.registerToolkit`. Use `HttpApiMcp.layerHttp(config)` to compose operation discovery, the generated client, tool registration, and the HTTP MCP server in one layer. Tool validation and payload transformations use the same schemas as the CLI and AI toolkit.

## Installation And Imports

Install `@krak-stack/registry` and use `@krak-stack/registry/httpapi/mcp`. If you instead install the `@krak-stack/httpapi-mcp` registry item with shadcn, replace that import with `@/lib/httpapi-mcp`. Keep application-specific handlers in your application with either installation method.

## Create An MCP Handler

Create `src/lib/mcp-handler.ts`:

```ts
import { Layer } from "effect";
import { FetchHttpClient } from "effect/http";
import { HttpApiMcp } from "@krak-stack/registry/httpapi/mcp";
import { AppApi } from "@/api";

export const mcpLayer = HttpApiMcp.layerHttp({
  api: AppApi,
  baseUrl: "http://localhost:3000",
}).pipe(Layer.provide(FetchHttpClient.layer));
```

Provide `mcpLayer` alongside the application's API routes in its HTTP runtime. The factory defaults to `/api/mcp` and GET operations only. Set `path`, `methods`, `include`, or `toolMetaKey` when needed. Annotate the API with `OpenApi.annotations({ title, version, description })`; its title and version identify the MCP server and CLI.

## Inputs And Results

Tool arguments use `{ params, query, headers, body }`. For a numeric query, use `{ "query": { "days": 7 } }`, not a string. The generated client performs wire encoding. Native MCP registration handles result formatting and validation errors.

## Authentication And Write Operations

Provide an app-owned authenticated `HttpClient` layer instead of `FetchHttpClient.layer` when the downstream API requires credentials. Inbound MCP authentication, authorization, telemetry, and deployment routing remain application-owned. Never expose credentials as tool arguments.

MCP registration disables toolkit approval requirements: it does not enforce the agent toolkit's write-approval policy. Keep the GET-only default unless you intentionally expose writes and enforce the required authorization or confirmation outside this adapter. Method annotations describe behavior; they are not access controls.

`HttpApiMcp.layer`, `httpApiMcpToolsLayer`, and `httpApiMcpServerLayer` remain available for custom composition.
